AB  - This paper presents a comprehensive comparative analysis of three distinct prompt engineering strategies—Zero-Shot, Few-Shot, and Chain-of-Thought—for Python code debugging applications using DeepSeek Coder 1.3B, a lightweight large language model. The study addresses the critical gap in understanding how different prompting approaches perform with compact models in resource-constrained environments, which is increasingly important for practical deployment scenarios. Through systematic evaluation on a custom dataset comprising 20 carefully curated Python debugging problems covering syntax errors, logic errors, and control cases, we employed a weighted evaluation metric that prioritizes functional correctness while accounting for syntax validity and execution capability. The experimental framework utilized automated testing and statistical analysis to ensure robust and reproducible results. Contrary to prevailing assumptions in the literature, our findings demonstrate that Zero-Shot prompting achieves superior performance with a mean score of 0.783, significantly outperforming Few-Shot (0.643) and Chain-of-Thought (0.613) approaches. Notably, Chain-of-Thought exhibits substantial performance variability (±0.369) and particularly struggles with syntax error correction, while Zero-Shot maintains consistent superiority across all bug types examined. These counterintuitive results suggest that for lightweight models, simpler prompting strategies enable more effective computational resource allocation to core debugging tasks. The findings have significant implications for developing practical debugging tools in educational settings and resource-constrained deployment environments, challenging the universal applicability of sophisticated prompting techniques across different model scales.
ER  - 

