AB  - To address the issues of numerical calculation instability and error-prone code generation in large models for text-to-table question answering, this paper proposes the PositiveNegative Chain-of-Thought (PN-CoT) framework. This framework constructs a pool of positive and negative examples using LLMs, and dynamically selects prompts for each question based on semantic similarity, combining “positive example demonstrations + negative example counterexamples.” It further integrates symbolic reasoning with external interpreters to handle computations, decoupling reasoning from calculation to enable numerical reasoning over text and tables. Compared to methods that rely on large-scale fine-tuning or are highly sensitive to prompt examples, PN -CoT matches suitable examples and execution paths to specific tasks, achieving higher accuracy, stability, and interpretability on FinQA and TAT-QA benchmarks.
