AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed for code generation from natural language prompts, yet the causal influence of individual prompt components on generated code features remains poorly understood. This paper presents a systematic prompt ablation study across three real-world REST API coding tasks—user registration, password reset, and product search—comparing Claude Sonnet 4.6 and GPT-4o. For each task, six semantic components are independently removed across seven prompt variants, yielding 42 experiment runs scored on five features each using an LLM-as-judge methodology augmented by manual code inspection. Our results validate and refine a five-type taxonomy of prompt-to-code relationships. Most critically, Claude exhibits proactive security—implementing secure practices without explicit instruction—while GPT-4o silently omits security-critical features without warning. Cross-model agreement averages 76.2% (95% CI [67.1%, 83.5%]), Cohen’s κ = 0.52 (moderate agreement), and output stability is 92.9% (Wilson CI [68.5%, 98.7%]), exceeding prior non-determinism estimates [13]. These findings have direct implications for responsible LLM deployment in production software engineering.
ER  - 

