AB  - We investigate prompt-bias—the measurable shift in code correctness, maintainability, and structural efficiency induced by different prompting strategies—via a controlled five-condition study on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct across 257 MBPP tasks, with cross-model validation on Mistral-7B and Phi-3.5-mini. Our core contribution is a dual-axis evaluation framework jointly measuring static maintainability (Cyclomatic Complexity, LOC, AST loop depth) and dynamic execution behavior, supported by bootstrap 95% CIs. Results show that Pythonic prompting dominates across both axes, achieving the highest correctness while minimizing structural complexity, and exhibits a novel Pythonic decoupling effect (r=-0.06, non-significant) where correctness becomes nearly uncorrelated with cyclomatic complexity. Zero-Verbosity prompting induces severe syntax-error cascades (34%), and Chain-of-Thought underperforms due to token-budget exhaustion at 8B scale—findings that provide actionable guidance for prompt design in resource-constrained deployments.
ER  - 

