AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used for industrial firmware code generation, yet the generated code often contains security vulnerabilities, posing serious risks in industrial firmware development. Prompt engineering offers a lightweight approach to improving code security; however, existing studies largely rely on vulnerability statistics and provide limited insight into why certain prompts are more effective, especially in resource-constrained and real-time firmware environments.In this paper, we examine how prompt design affects the security of LLM-generated industrial firmware code. We propose a structured prompt framework that decomposes security instructions into explicit, reusable components aligned with firmware-specific constraints. To quantify security risks, we introduce Risky Pattern Exposure (RPE), which measures the prevalence of security-sensitive coding patterns in generated code. Experiments on industrial firmware tasks across multiple LLMs show that structured security prompts significantly reduce security risks without compromising functional correctness. Our findings offer practical guidance for designing security-aware prompts in LLM-assisted firmware development.
