AB  - Frequent requirement changes in modern DevOps pipelines significantly increase the cost of test maintenance and often lead to insufficient test coverage. Although Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promise in automating test generation, existing approaches struggle with long, unstructured requirements due to context loss, logical hallucinations, and weak alignment between requirements and executable tests. Recent empirical studies further indicate that LLM-based test generation remains sensitive to prompt design, execution context, and validation mechanisms.This paper proposes ScenarioLLM, a scenario-driven automated test generation framework that integrates structured scenario modeling with multi-stage Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning. The framework first transforms natural language requirements into formalized scenario tuples through hierarchical scenario extraction. It then employs a structured CoT prompting strategy to explicitly reason about system states, boundary conditions, and exception flows before generating test scripts. To improve robustness and executability, ScenarioLLM incorporates a static-analysis-guided self-correction loop inspired by recent work on iterative refinement and validation of LLM-generated tests.Experiments conducted on an e-commerce platform and an intelligent customer service system demonstrate that ScenarioLLM significantly improves test executability (Pass@3 up to 96.8%) and exception-scenario coverage (up to 79.3%) compared with baseline approaches, while substantially reducing human debugging effort in CI/CD pipelines. These results indicate that combining scenario modeling with explicit reasoning and feedback mechanisms is an effective pathway toward reliable, requirement-aligned test automation in DevOps environments.
