AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used to generate source code from natural-language descriptions. However, LLMs are predominantly trained on English data. This imbalance raises the question of whether prompt language affects the quality of generated code. We examined how English and Spanish prompts influence the functional correctness of LLMgenerated code. We evaluated three contemporary reasoningcapable models, GPT-5, Gemini 2.5 Pro, and DeepSeek V3.2, on the multilingual HumanEval-XL benchmark using Pass@1. Across models and programming languages, English prompts generally yielded slightly higher correctness scores, although Spanish prompts matched or exceeded English in twelve programming languages. Among the evaluated models, GPT-5 was the most consistent top performer with the smallest English-Spanish gap. Our results suggested that model choice and programming language had a larger impact on correctness than prompt language. Compared with prior reports, this EnglishSpanish gap appears to be narrowing in current-generation models.
