AB  - Large Language Models have emerged as the top-notch tool in the software engineering field, from requirement gathering and analysis to code generation. Several approaches have been developed to apply artificial intelligence in software testing, from using the fine-tuned language models in documentation parsing to test case generation. However, these approaches require special tools and interfaces, which can take time to adopt in real-world software testing. In this paper, we aim to explore the capacity and performance of prevalent Large Language Models in generating API test cases and import them to existing testing tools because API testing is one of the core and earliest phases to ensure the quality and matching requirements of developed software. Natural language processing and code generation capabilities of four state-of-the-art LLMs, Grok, Claude, Gemini, and GPT, are applied to generate API test cases, covering the path, parameter, and response checking coverage, which are the main features of API testing. It was discovered that native LLMs can be applied in API testing effectively, given the API document and prompted with clear and concise information. All models under study can properly understand the natural language description in the API document and call the endpoint with appropriate parameter values according to the description. Among all four models, Grok and Claude showed the best performance with Chain-of-Thought Prompting approach.
