AB  - Financial question answering requires multi-hop numerical reasoning over dense text and tables, making it particularly challenging for aggressively quantized Small Language Models (SLMs). Although integrating Chain-of-Thought (CoT) before Program-of-Thought (PoT) may improve information extraction before code execution, its effectiveness under 4-bit compression remains unclear because longer reasoning traces may introduce additional generation burden and make executable code generation more brittle. This study presents a controlled ablation comparing Pure PoT and single-pass CoT+PoT across five 4-bit quantized SLMs ranging from 3.8B to 8B parameters on the FinQA benchmark. Both configurations are fine-tuned using QLoRA with identical adapter settings to ensure a fair and controlled comparison. The results show that CoT integration is not universally beneficial under compressed inference. It yields modest accuracy gains for Llama-3-8B (67.48% to 67.83%) and Qwen2.5-Coder-7B (65.74% to 66.52%), but reduces Mistral-7B performance (63.12% to 61.29%) and causes complete execution collapse in Phi-3.8B and DeepSeek-Math-7B. Error profiling further reveals a key trade-off: CoT can reduce logical reasoning errors in stronger architectures, but it also increases execution failures by making Python generation more syntactically brittle. These findings indicate that under 4-bit quantization, the effectiveness of CoT+PoT is conditional across model backbones and may be shaped by architectural specialization, executable-generation robustness, and quantization sensitivity rather than parameter count alone.
ER  - 

