AB  - Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have accelerated research in hardware design and verification. A critical stage in LLM-assisted hardware development is generating accurate, efficient Hardware Description Language (HDL) code. The effectiveness of this process strongly depends on the quality and representation of datasets used for model training and fine-tuning. Although several studies have proposed various LLM architectures, code generation strategies, and verification methodologies for HDL generation, the impact of dataset descriptions on model performance has been insufficiently explored. Existing studies primarily investigate Prompt-of-Thought (PoT) descriptions for Verilog datasets, demonstrating that such prompting strategies can influence the accuracy of Verilog code generation. However, the effect of natural language (NL) descriptions on VHDL dataset fine-tuning and VHDL code generation has not been thoroughly analyzed. To address this research gap, this paper investigates the effect of natural language descriptions on fine-tuned LLMs for VHDL code generation. In particular, the study analyzes how augmenting VHDL datasets with additional natural-language descriptions affects the accuracy and quality of the generated VHDL code. Experimental evaluations are conducted on VHDL datasets using three state-of-the-art LLMs: Code Llama, Qwen, and DeepSeek. The experimental results demonstrate that incorporating enhanced natural-language descriptions into VHDL datasets improves the accuracy and overall quality of the generated VHDL code. These findings highlight the importance of dataset description design in improving the effectiveness of LLM-based HDL code generation frameworks.
ER  - 

