AB  - This research explores the capabilities of large language models (LLMs) in the context of additive manufacturing, with a focus on generating G-code from natural language prompts. Three models were evaluated: StarCoder2-3B, Mistral-7B, and an instruction-tuned variant of Mistral-7B. The study began with the creation of a custom JSONL dataset containing curated G-code samples for 15 objects, which was used to fine-tune StarCoder2-3B via SFTTrainer. Due to token limitations encountered during fine-tuning, the process was shifted to Mistral-7B, where a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) approach was implemented using a knowledge base constructed from G-code manuals and reference guides. Subsequently, an instruction-tuned version of Mistral-7B was employed, supported by the development of a new promptcompletion dataset tailored to the task. Among the three approaches, the instruction-tuned Mistral-7B produced the most promising results, though both Mistral-based methods introduced a significant amount of noise and irrelevant outputs. The findings suggest that with sufficient computational resources and a well-structured dataset, prompt-to-G-code generation is a viable direction–particularly beneficial for practitioners in CNC machining and 3D printing workflows.
ER  - 

