AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong performance in code generation, the quality of the generated output is highly dependent on the format of the prompt. The instructions sent by the users are, in most cases, not sufficient or unclear and extract different or unreliable responses. Our proposed paper proposes a six-layer promptrefinement framework that plays the role of the systematic transformation of naive prompts into formalized task specifications. “We further introduce a Prompt Quality Score (PQS) to measure structural completeness independent of downstream code correctness.” We cast prompt refinement as a conditional sequence generation problem, solved using parameter-efficient LoRA finetuning on TinyLlama-1.1B. The results of the experiment show that the convergence is stable, with training loss is reduced to 0.37, “strong semantic alignment is also preserved” (BERTScore = 0.8505), and the structural improvement is significant as the mean PQS grew up to 0.96. These results suggest learnability of structural augmentation patterns which can be quantified based on formal structural measures, forming a measurable basis on systematic prompt engineering.
ER  - 

